      "For example: $100 → $200 → $300 → $400 → $500. The portfolio is growing because you're continuing to contribute. Then, if your investments generate returns, those returns can add another layer of growth. That's the basic idea behind: **contributions + time + potential growth.**",
      "## The Power of Consistent Contributions",
      "Let's use a simple example. Suppose you invest $100 every month. Over one year, you would contribute $1,200. After five years: $6,000. After ten years: $12,000. After twenty years: $24,000. After thirty years: $36,000. That's the money you personally contributed. But what happens if those investments grow over time? That's where compounding can make a significant difference.",
      "## What Could $100 a Month Become?",
      "Let's use a hypothetical example. Suppose you invest $100 every month for 30 years and your investments earn an average annual return of 7%, compounded monthly. You would contribute $36,000. Over the 30 years, the hypothetical portfolio could grow to approximately $122,000. That means approximately: $36,000 = your contributions, ~$86,000 = hypothetical investment growth.",
      "This is only an illustration. A 7% annual return isn't guaranteed. Real investment returns fluctuate from year to year, and fees, taxes, inflation and market conditions can affect actual results. The purpose of the example is to demonstrate the potential effect of **time and compounding.**",
      "## What If You Start With More?",
      "Now imagine increasing your monthly contribution.",
      "| Monthly Contribution | 30-Year Contributions | Hypothetical Value at 7% |\n|---|---|---|\n| $50 | $18,000 | ~$61,000 |\n| $100 | $36,000 | ~$122,000 |\n| $250 | $90,000 | ~$305,000 |\n| $500 | $180,000 | ~$610,000 |\n| $1,000 | $360,000 | ~$1.22 million |",
      "*Illustration assumes monthly contributions and a constant 7% annual return. Actual investment results will vary.*",
      "The point isn't that you need to invest $1,000 a month. The point is that your contribution amount and the amount of time your money remains invested can both have a major impact on the potential outcome.",
      "## Time Can Be Your Biggest Advantage",
      "Consider two investors. **Investor A** starts investing at age 25 with $100 per month. **Investor B** waits until age 40 and invests $300 per month. Investor B is investing three times as much every month. But Investor A has something Investor B can't buy: **fifteen additional years.**",
      "This is why starting early can be so powerful. You cannot go back and invest the money you didn't invest five or ten years ago. But you can decide what you do with the money you have **today**.",
      "## Don't Wait Until You Have \"Enough\"",
      "This is one of the biggest mistakes beginners can make. They tell themselves: \"I'll start investing when I make more money.\" Then they get a raise. And say: \"I'll start when I pay off a few more things.\" Then another expense appears. There is always another reason to wait.",
      "Of course, you should take care of essential expenses and your overall financial stability first. But if you have money available for long-term investing, you don't necessarily need to wait until you have thousands of dollars. **Starting with $50 or $100 can be a perfectly reasonable way to begin learning.** The amount can grow later.",
      "## Your First $100 Is More Than $100",
      "Your first $100 investment may not make a dramatic difference to your net worth. But it can change your behavior. It teaches you how to: open and use an investment account, place an investment order, understand what you own, track your contributions, watch market movements, understand gains and losses, learn about dividends, understand your account type, and build an investing routine.",
      "That's why your first $100 can be important. **You're not just investing money. You're investing in your financial education.**",
      "## Consistency Can Matter More Than Perfection",
      "Many new investors spend a lot of time trying to figure out the perfect investment. But there's another question that's just as important: can I consistently invest over the next 10, 20 or 30 years? A perfect strategy that you abandon after six months isn't very useful. A simple strategy that you understand and can maintain may be much easier to stick with.",
      "That's one reason regular contributions can be powerful. For example: every payday → $50 invested. If you're paid twice a month: $50 × 2 = $100/month. Over time, that routine can become part of your financial life.",
      "## What Happens When Your Income Increases?",
      "Starting small doesn't mean you have to stay small. Imagine you begin with $100/month. A few years later, your income increases. You increase your contribution to $150/month. Later: $200/month. Then: $300/month. Your investment contribution grows alongside your financial capacity. This can be easier than trying to start with a large monthly contribution immediately.",
      "## Compound Growth Isn't the Same as Guaranteed Growth",
