      "For example, a TFSA can hold investments and generally allows investment income and capital gains to grow tax-free. But your personal TFSA contribution room depends on your individual circumstances. That's why understanding and tracking your contribution room is important.",
      "## Why Tracking Matters From Day One",
      "You might think: \"My portfolio is only $100. Why would I need a tracker?\" Because someday it might not be $100. It might be $1,000. Then $10,000. Then $50,000. Then potentially much more.",
      "As your portfolio grows, so does the amount of information you need to keep track of. You may eventually have: multiple accounts, multiple brokerages, multiple investments, dividend income, different purchase prices, multiple transactions, withdrawals, contributions, ACB calculations, and capital gains. That's why building the tracking habit early can be useful.",
      "## Your Brokerage Shows You Your Account",
      "Your brokerage is where you buy and sell your investments. But as your portfolio becomes more complicated, you may want a broader view. That's where MapleSheet comes in. MapleSheet is designed to help Canadian DIY investors organize and understand their investment information.",
      "You can track things such as: contributions, holdings, dividends, purchase prices, average cost, ACB, portfolio performance, investment income, account types, and overall portfolio progress. Instead of simply asking \"How much is my portfolio worth?\" you can start asking: \"How much have I contributed?\" \"How much has my portfolio grown?\" \"How much dividend income am I receiving?\" \"What's my average cost?\" \"How is each account performing?\" That's the difference between simply owning investments and understanding your investments.",
      "## Start With What You Can Afford",
      "There's no universal \"correct\" amount to invest every month. For one person, it might be $25. For another: $100. For someone else: $500. The right amount is the amount that fits your financial situation and doesn't interfere with essential expenses or financial obligations. Don't invest money you know you'll need for your immediate expenses. And don't feel pressured to match someone else's investment amount. **Your financial journey is your own.**",
      "## Small Steps Can Become a Big Journey",
      "Imagine looking back 20 years from now. Would you rather say \"I wish I had started\" or \"I'm glad I started with what I could afford\"? You can't control what the market will do tomorrow. You can't guarantee what your investments will be worth in 20 years. But you can control: when you start, how much you contribute, how consistently you invest, how much you learn, and how carefully you track your progress.",
      "## The MapleSheet Approach",
      "At MapleSheet, we believe investing doesn't have to begin with a huge portfolio. It can begin with a simple decision: start. Then: invest, track, learn, repeat. As your income and knowledge grow, your contributions can grow too. And while compound growth isn't guaranteed, giving your investments more time can give them more opportunity to potentially grow.",
      "## Your First Investment Doesn't Have to Be Big",
