      "That's why a DCA investor needs to understand something: falling prices aren't automatically a reason to panic. If you've chosen an investment because you believe it is appropriate for your long-term strategy, temporary market declines may simply be part of the journey. However, if a market decline causes you to realize that you cannot tolerate the investment's risk, that's something you should take seriously.",
      "## DCA vs. Trying to Time the Market",
      "**Investor A — Market Timer:** \"I'll invest when the market falls.\" The problem? You don't know when the bottom will happen. The market could fall 5%. You wait. It falls another 5%. You wait. Then the market suddenly rebounds. Now you wonder: \"Should I buy?\" You can easily end up waiting for a perfect opportunity that never arrives.",
      "**Investor B — Consistent Investor:** \"I'll invest $100 every month.\" Market up? Invest $100. Market down? Invest $100. Market sideways? Invest $100. The strategy doesn't require you to predict tomorrow's market.",
      "## What Happens When the Market Falls?",
      "Suppose you invest $100 per month.",
