      "## But Is DCA Always Better?",
      "No. This is an important distinction. If you already have a large amount of money available to invest, investing it immediately may produce better expected returns than spreading the investment out over time, because the money has more time in the market. Research comparing lump-sum investing with staged investing has generally found that lump-sum investing has a higher expected return over long periods because markets have historically risen more often than they have fallen.",
      "But that doesn't mean lump-sum investing is automatically the best choice for every person. Why? Because investing isn't purely mathematical. **Behavior matters.** If investing your entire $20,000 today causes you to panic and sell when the market falls, a more gradual approach might help you stay invested. A strategy you can actually stick with can be more useful than a theoretically optimal strategy that you abandon.",
      "## DCA and Your Paycheque",
      "One of the easiest ways to implement DCA is through regular income. Imagine you're paid every two weeks. You decide $50 from every paycheque goes toward investing. There are approximately 26 biweekly pay periods in a year. That's $50 × 26 = $1,300 per year — without needing to make a large investment decision every month. You could increase that amount later, for example $50 → $75 → $100 per paycheque as your financial situation improves.",
      "## What If You Can Only Invest $25?",
